1. Core Architecture OverviewTo support real-time discovery, booking, and tracking, you need three major endpoints interacting with a central backend:[Customer Mobile App]   <-- REST / WebSocket -->   [Backend Services]   <-- REST / WebSocket -->   [Provider Mobile App]
                                                           |
                                                    [Admin Dashboard]
Customer Client: Location discovery, provider browsing/filtering, order lifecycle, live map tracking, payment checkout.Provider Client: Go online/offline toggle, incoming job dispatch radar (accept/reject countdown), route navigation to customer, status progression triggers.Backend Core: Geospatial querying (PostGIS / Redis Geospatial), WebSocket gateway for bidirectional low-latency location streams, job state machine, payment escrow/payout rails.Admin Dashboard: Dispute handling, manual dispatch override, KYC/verification approval, commission and payout monitoring.2. Feature & Component BreakdownA. Discovery & Geospatial Indexing ("see list of available pple nd prices")Provider Presence & Availability:Provider app periodically pings current (lat, lng) when set to Available.Keep dynamic coordinates in memory using Redis Geospatial Indexing (GEOADD, GEORADIUS / GEOSEARCH) with a TTL. Pushing every 3–5-second coordinate update directly into PostgreSQL will choke your database under write contention.Geofenced Querying:Client sends device location; backend runs a radial search (e.g., within a 5 km or 10 km radius) filtered by service_category and is_active = true.Return metadata: base fee, hourly/service rate, provider rating, estimated arrival time (ETA calculated via distance matrix API).B. Booking Lifecycle & Dispatch Engine ("den book")Job State Machine:
A strict finite-state machine (FSM) is critical so orders don't hang in limbo:PENDING_ACCEPTANCE ➔ ACCEPTED ➔ ON_ROUTE ➔ ARRIVED ➔ IN_PROGRESS ➔ COMPLETED ➔ PAID
       |                  |
       └── CANCELLED ◄────┘
Dispatch Strategies:Direct Booking: Customer picks an exact provider from the map/list; request sends a push notification/modal to that provider with a 30–45s accept timer.Broadcast/Pool Dispatch (True Bolt style): Customer confirms service type; system pings the nearest 3–5 providers sequentially until one accepts.C. Live Movement & Map Tracking ("Track movement from dere place to locate u")Real-time Pipeline:Provider App: Emits background location coordinates using a foreground service (Android) / background location permission (iOS) via WebSockets (or MQTT / Pusher / Ably).Backend Gateway: Throttles/batches events (e.g., emit once every 3–5 seconds or after moving at least 10 meters) and publishes to a dedicated channel: job:{id}:tracking.Customer App: Subscribes to job:{id}:tracking, interpolates provider marker smooth transitions across map coordinates, and polls Google Directions API / Mapbox Directions API for live turn-by-turn polyline updates and remaining ETA.D. Trust, Payments & SettlementsEscrow Hold: Pre-authorize card or hold funds in a platform escrow wallet when booking is initiated; release to provider wallet (minus platform take-rate/commission) only when customer or system confirms job completion.Proof of Service: OTP exchange (customer gives a 4-digit code to provider upon arrival to initiate IN_PROGRESS) prevents fake check-ins.3. Recommended Tech StackLayerRecommended ChoiceWhyMobile AppsFlutter or React NativeSingle codebase for iOS & Android with mature background location plugins (flutter_background_geolocation or react-native-background-actions).Backend FrameworkNode.js (NestJS / Fastify) or GoHigh-throughput asynchronous event loops suited for continuous WebSocket traffic and lightweight connection pools.Real-time TransportRedis Pub/Sub + WebSockets (Socket.io) or Managed (Ably / Pusher)Real-time coordinate relay without overloading database connections.DatabasePostgreSQL + PostGISMaster record storage for user tables, transactions, reviews, and complex static polygon geofencing.Location CacheRedisFast ephemeral spatial queries (GEOSEARCH) for live provider location polling.Maps / RoutingMapbox or Google Maps SDKMap rendering, routing, distance matrix calculations, and turn polylines.4. Technical Traps to Flag to the Client EarlyOS Battery Optimizations: Both iOS and modern Android aggressively kill background apps. Capturing smooth GPS traces while the provider switches to phone calls or screen-off requires configuring persistent foreground services with active notification channels.Third-party Map API Costs: Querying the Google Directions API every 3 seconds for turn navigation will burn through cloud credits instantly. The backend should only route map polylines on initial accept and route deviations; point-to-point updates on the customer side should use local map client bearing calculations.Cancellation & Dead Mileage: A provider rides 15 minutes to an address, and the customer cancels. You need automated cancellation fee windows and compensation logic.